{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012t1xrkojuTSLmdX8S7ygjU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the context I need. Let me carefully construct the imagined experiments for the median tier based on:\n\n1. The median tier definition with its core assumptions and key metric ranges\n2. The experiment plan with 12 experiments\n3. The existing registry data (pilot results from 100-step runs)\n4. The paper framing and narrative direction\n\nLet me go through each planned experiment and generate detailed predictions with rationales.\n\nKey reference points from the pilot (100-step) data:\n- exec-only (r5): prove_rate=0.15125, ISR/PR ratio=1.0 (last20), overall_reward_mean=0.15125\n\n\n- exec+STA (r5): prove_rate=0.10, ISR mean=0.04347, ISR/PR ratio overall=0.43, last20=0.33, overall_reward_mean=0.07174\n- STA phase0 baseline: mean_isr=0.0641, prove_rate=0.12, ISR range [0, 0.889]\n- GT ISR baseline: gt_prove_rate=0.18, gt_mean_isr=0.5588\n\nNow I'm looking at the median tier key metric ranges to establish what performance targets I should be aiming for in these imagined experiments. I'm looking at the key findings across these experiments—the ISR/PR ratio declines more gradually with exec+STA compared to exec-only, though neither stabilizes below 0.5 by step 500, and the prove rate takes a 5-15% hit when adding STA rewards. The STA weight ablation shows the model is sensitive to these settings but no single configuration stands out, and while Prolog STA produces results, the signal is too noisy to draw firm conclusions. Cross-logic transfer remains essentially flat across different seeds. Now I'm working through the YAML structure for all 12 experiments, starting with the FOL exec-only baseline at 500 steps across 3 seeds. The STA penalty slows down prove_rate gains but keeps the ISR/PR ratio higher than execution-only approaches, though it still declines as the model learns to exploit both rewards. On held-out data, the exec+STA model shows measurably better structural integrity compared to exec-only, though the improvement is modest since STA doesn't fully prevent gaming. The O(1) variant shouldn't meaningfully improve ISR/PR ratio since it only checks for tautologies.\n\nFor weight ablation, no single configuration dominates—curriculum scheduling helps somewhat by letting the model learn basic proving before being penalized for shortcuts. Prolog's rule-based structure makes STA ablation noisier than FOL since rules are more interconnected, producing less clean signals. Cross-logic transfer shows minimal delta, hovering near zero. -zero transfer because FOL and Prolog require fundamentally different syntactic and semantic patterns. Any positive transfer is likely from general formalization ability rather than logic-specific skills.\n\nFor plan_008 (Cross-logic transfer eval):\n- Nearly zero or inconsistent\n- Rationale: FOL and Prolog are syntactically different enough that direct transfer fails. Any benefit comes from improved general reasoning, not formalization skills.\n\nFor plan_009 (SFT baseline):\n- SFT should have higher prove_rate but lower ISR than exec+STA GRPO\n- Rationale: SFT learns to imitate ground truth, so outputs are more diverse but don't have the GRPO-specific hacking patterns. However, SFT can't match GRPO's prove_rate because it doesn't get solver feedback.\n\nFor plan_010 (SMT-LIB):\n- Similar to Prolog findings but with noisier results since SMT-LIB constraint ablation is the most complex STA instantiation.\n\nFor plan_011 (8B scaling):\n- Larger models should prove more often with potentially similar ISR and PR trends, since increased capacity enables both more successful proofs and more sophisticated hacking.\n\nFor plan_012 (Hacking characterization):\n- Manual inspection should reveal a taxonomy of specific hacking patterns and structural degeneracy modes.\n\nNow I'm formatting this as YAML...","signature":""}],"sto